var e={level:`B2`,type:`verb-form`,questions:[{id:`b2-vf-001`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`past-perfect`,prompt:`By the time we arrived, the film ___.`,explanation:`Use the past perfect (had + past participle) for an action before another past action.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The past simple does not show the earlier action.`,2:`'has started' is present perfect, but the sentence is in the past.`,3:`'was starting' shows an action in progress, not a completed earlier action.`},verb:`already / start`,options:[`already started`,`had already started`,`has already started`,`was already starting`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-002`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`I wish I ___ speak French.`,explanation:`After 'wish' about the present, use the past form: I wish I could speak French.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'wish' the verb moves back in time: can becomes could.`,2:`'would' is used for other people's behaviour, not for ability.`,3:`'will' is not used after wish.`},verb:`can`,options:[`can`,`could`,`would`,`will`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-003`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`If I had known you were vegetarian, I ___ a different dish last night.`,explanation:`Third conditional: if + past perfect, would have + past participle. If I had known, I would have cooked something else.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'would cook' talks about the present or future, but 'last night' is in the past.`,2:`'had cooked' belongs in the if-clause, not in the result clause.`,3:`'will have cooked' is the future perfect and is not used for an unreal past result.`},verb:`cook`,options:[`would cook`,`would have cooked`,`had cooked`,`will have cooked`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-004`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`If the bus ___ on time, we wouldn't have missed the start of the concert.`,explanation:`In a third conditional the if-clause uses the past perfect: If the bus had come on time, we wouldn't have missed it.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The past simple is for unreal present situations; this is about a past event, so use the past perfect.`,2:`We do not normally use 'would' in the if-clause of a conditional.`,3:`The present perfect cannot be used in a third conditional.`},verb:`come`,options:[`had come`,`came`,`would have come`,`has come`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-005`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`If she ___ more confidence in herself, she would apply for the manager's job.`,explanation:`Second conditional: if + past simple, would + base form, for unreal present situations. If she had more time, she would help.`,wrongReasons:{0:`With 'has' the result would need 'will', not 'would'.`,2:`'had had' is for the past and would need 'would have applied' in the result.`,3:`'would' is not used in the if-clause of a second conditional.`},verb:`have`,options:[`has`,`had`,`had had`,`would have`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-006`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Unless you ___ the form by Friday, your application will not be accepted.`,explanation:`'Unless' means 'if not' and takes the present simple when talking about the future: Unless you hurry, you'll miss the bus.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'unless' we use the present simple for the future, not 'will'.`,1:`'Unless' already means 'if not', so a negative verb gives the wrong meaning.`,3:`The past simple does not match 'will' in the main clause.`},verb:`sign`,options:[`will sign`,`don't sign`,`sign`,`signed`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-007`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`If you heat ice, it ___.`,explanation:`Zero conditional: if + present simple, present simple, for facts that are always true. If you heat water to 100°C, it boils.`,wrongReasons:{0:`This is a general scientific fact, so it is a zero conditional, not an imaginary situation.`,2:`The past simple does not fit a general truth in the present.`,3:`The present perfect is not used to state what always happens.`},verb:`melt`,options:[`would melt`,`melts`,`melted`,`has melted`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-008`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`If I ___ you, I would talk to the landlord before signing anything.`,explanation:`We give advice with 'If I were you, I would...'. If I were you, I would take the job.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'If I am you' is impossible; giving advice with 'would' needs the unreal form 'were'.`,1:`'had been' refers to the past and would need 'would have talked'.`,3:`'would' does not go in the if-clause.`},verb:`be`,options:[`am`,`had been`,`were`,`would be`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-009`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`If he had taken the job in Berlin, he ___ in Germany now.`,explanation:`Mixed conditional: a past condition (had + past participle) with a present result (would + verb). If he had studied, he would be a doctor now.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'now' shows a present result, so 'would have lived' (a past result) does not fit.`,2:`'will' is for real future situations, but this one is imaginary.`,3:`The past perfect belongs in the if-clause, not the result.`},verb:`live`,options:[`would be living`,`would have lived`,`will be living`,`had lived`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-010`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`If I weren't so afraid of flying, I ___ to Australia with my friends last year.`,explanation:`Mixed conditional: a present situation (weren't) with a past result (would have gone). If I weren't so shy, I would have spoken to her yesterday.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'last year' is in the past, so the result needs 'would have + past participle'.`,1:`The past perfect is used in the if-clause, not in the result clause.`,2:`The past simple states a real fact, but this trip never happened.`},verb:`go`,options:[`would go`,`had gone`,`went`,`would have gone`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-011`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`We'll miss the train unless we ___ now.`,explanation:`After 'unless' (= if not) use the present simple for the future: We'll be late unless we take a taxi.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'will' is not used after 'unless' to talk about the future.`,2:`The past simple does not match the real future situation in this sentence.`,3:`'unless' already contains the idea of 'not', so a negative verb is wrong here.`},verb:`leave`,options:[`will leave`,`leave`,`left`,`don't leave`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-012`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Provided that the weather ___ good, the festival will take place in the park.`,explanation:`'Provided that' works like 'if' and takes the present simple for the future: Provided that you pay on time, there's no extra fee.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'provided that' we use the present simple, not 'will', for a future condition.`,1:`The past simple would suit an imaginary situation with 'would', not 'will'.`,3:`'would' does not belong in the condition clause.`},verb:`stay`,options:[`will stay`,`stayed`,`stays`,`would stay`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-013`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`If the engineers had checked the bridge properly, the accident ___.`,explanation:`Third conditional result: would (not) have + past participle. If we had left earlier, we wouldn't have been late.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The accident is in the past, so we need 'wouldn't have + past participle'.`,1:`The past perfect is for the if-clause, not the result clause.`,3:`'won't have' is the future perfect and does not fit an unreal past.`},verb:`not happen`,options:[`wouldn't happen`,`hadn't happened`,`wouldn't have happened`,`won't have happened`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-014`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`If you ___ the red button, the machine stops immediately.`,explanation:`Zero conditional for things that always happen: if + present simple, present simple. If you mix red and blue, you get purple.`,wrongReasons:{1:`With the past simple the result would need 'would stop'.`,2:`'will' is not used in the if-clause.`,3:`'would' is not used in the if-clause of a zero conditional.`},verb:`press`,options:[`press`,`pressed`,`will press`,`would press`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-015`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Supposing you ___ the lottery, what would you do with the money?`,explanation:`'Supposing' works like 'if' in a second conditional: Supposing you lost your job, what would you do?`,wrongReasons:{0:`The question uses 'would', so the condition must be in the past simple to show it is imaginary.`,1:`'had won' is for the past and would need 'would have done'.`,3:`'would' does not go in the condition clause after 'supposing'.`},verb:`win`,options:[`win`,`had won`,`won`,`would win`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-016`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`I would have called you if my phone ___ out of battery.`,explanation:`Third conditional: the if-clause uses the past perfect. I would have come if I hadn't been ill.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The past simple is for unreal present situations; the call is in the past.`,1:`'would' belongs in the result clause, not after 'if'.`,3:`The present perfect is not used in a third conditional.`},verb:`not run`,options:[`didn't run`,`wouldn't have run`,`hadn't run`,`hasn't run`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-017`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`If it ___ tomorrow, we will hold the meeting online.`,explanation:`First conditional: if + present simple, will + verb. If it rains tomorrow, we will stay at home.`,wrongReasons:{0:`In a first conditional, the if-clause uses the present simple, not 'will'.`,1:`The past simple would need 'would' in the result clause.`,2:`'would' never goes in this if-clause.`},verb:`snow`,options:[`will snow`,`snowed`,`would snow`,`snows`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-018`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Had the referee seen the foul, he ___ a penalty.`,explanation:`'Had + subject + past participle' is a formal third conditional without 'if'. Had I known, I would have helped.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Had the referee seen' refers to the past, so the result needs 'would have given'.`,2:`The past perfect is already in the first clause; the result needs 'would have'.`,3:`The past simple says it really happened, but he did not see the foul.`},verb:`give`,options:[`would give`,`would have given`,`had given`,`gave`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-019`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`If our team ___ the final next week, the whole city will celebrate.`,explanation:`Use the present simple after 'if' to talk about a real future possibility: If she passes, she will go to university.`,wrongReasons:{1:`Do not use 'will' after 'if' in a first conditional.`,2:`The past simple would make the result 'would celebrate'.`,3:`'would' is not used in this if-clause.`},verb:`win`,options:[`wins`,`will win`,`won`,`would win`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-020`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`As long as you ___ your homework first, you can go to the cinema tonight.`,explanation:`'As long as' means 'only if' and takes the present simple: You can borrow my bike as long as you return it.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'as long as' is followed by the present simple, not 'will', for the future.`,2:`'would' does not go after 'as long as' in a real condition.`,3:`The past simple does not match the real future situation with 'can'.`},verb:`finish`,options:[`will finish`,`finish`,`would finish`,`finished`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-021`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`If I had saved more money when I was younger, I ___ worried about paying the rent now.`,explanation:`Mixed conditional: past condition + present result. If I had learned to drive, I wouldn't need the bus now.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'now' shows the result is in the present, so use 'would + base form'.`,2:`'won't' is for real future results, but this situation is imaginary.`,3:`The past perfect is used in the if-clause, not in the result.`},verb:`not be`,options:[`wouldn't be`,`wouldn't have been`,`won't be`,`hadn't been`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-022`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`In case it ___, take an umbrella with you.`,explanation:`'In case' + present simple means 'because it is possible that': Take some water in case you get thirsty.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'in case' we use the present simple, not 'will'.`,1:`'would' does not follow 'in case' in a real future situation.`,2:`The past simple does not match the instruction about the future.`},verb:`rain`,options:[`will rain`,`would rain`,`rained`,`rains`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-023`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`If the museum ___ on Mondays, we would visit it today, but it's closed.`,explanation:`Second conditional for unreal present situations: if + past simple, would + verb. If the shop opened on Sundays, I would go there today.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple suits a real condition, but 'would' and 'it's closed' show this is imaginary.`,1:`The past perfect is for the past, but 'today' shows the present.`,3:`'would' is not used in the if-clause.`},verb:`open`,options:[`opens`,`had opened`,`opened`,`would open`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-024`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`I wish I ___ so much cake at the party last night.`,explanation:`Use wish + past perfect to regret something in the past. I wish I hadn't said that.`,wrongReasons:{1:`For regrets about the past, 'wish' needs the past perfect, not the past simple.`,2:`'wish + would' is for complaints about other people's behaviour, not past regrets.`,3:`The present perfect is not used after 'wish'.`},verb:`not eat`,options:[`hadn't eaten`,`didn't eat`,`wouldn't eat`,`haven't eaten`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-025`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`She wishes she ___ closer to her office; the commute takes two hours.`,explanation:`Use wish + past simple for an unreal present situation. I wish I had a bigger flat.`,wrongReasons:{1:`After 'wish' a present situation moves back to the past simple.`,2:`We do not use 'would' after 'wish' when the subject is the same person.`,3:`The past perfect is for regrets about the past, but the long commute is now.`},verb:`live`,options:[`lived`,`lives`,`would live`,`had lived`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-026`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`I wish my neighbours ___ their music so loud every night.`,explanation:`Use wish + would to complain about someone else's annoying behaviour. I wish you wouldn't leave your shoes there.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple is not used after 'wish'.`,1:`The past perfect refers to one past event, not an annoying habit that continues.`,2:`'will' is never used after 'wish'.`},verb:`not play`,options:[`don't play`,`hadn't played`,`won't play`,`wouldn't play`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-027`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`If only I ___ the answer to her question yesterday!`,explanation:`'If only' + past perfect expresses a strong regret about the past. If only I had listened to you!`,wrongReasons:{0:`'yesterday' shows a past regret, so the past perfect is needed.`,1:`The present simple is not used after 'if only'.`,3:`'would' is not used for regrets about the past.`},verb:`know`,options:[`knew`,`know`,`had known`,`would know`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-028`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`I wish I ___ a car; then I wouldn't need to take the bus.`,explanation:`Wish + past simple talks about the present: I wish I had more free time.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'wish' the present situation needs the past simple, not the present.`,1:`We do not use 'would' after 'I wish I'.`,2:`'had had' is a past regret, but the sentence is about now.`},verb:`have`,options:[`have`,`would have`,`had had`,`had`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-029`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`I wish you ___ interrupting me when I'm speaking.`,explanation:`Wish + would + verb shows that someone's behaviour annoys us: I wish he would stop shouting.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'will' is not used after 'wish'.`,2:`The past perfect is a regret about the past, not a request about present behaviour.`,3:`The present continuous is not used after 'wish'.`},verb:`stop`,options:[`would stop`,`will stop`,`had stopped`,`are stopping`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-030`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`If only the sun ___ out! We've been waiting all day to go to the beach.`,explanation:`Use 'if only' + would for something we want to change but cannot control. If only the bus would arrive!`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple is not used after 'if only'.`,2:`'will' is not used after 'if only'.`,3:`The past perfect would be a regret about the past, but we are still waiting.`},verb:`come`,options:[`comes`,`would come`,`will come`,`had come`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-031`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`My grandfather wishes he ___ harder at school when he was a boy.`,explanation:`Wish + past perfect for regrets about the past: She wishes she had travelled more when she was young.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'when he was a boy' is the past, so the regret needs the past perfect.`,1:`'would' is not used for regrets about the past.`,2:`The present perfect is not used after 'wish'.`},verb:`study`,options:[`studied`,`would study`,`has studied`,`had studied`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-032`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`I wish I ___ work tomorrow; I'd love a day off.`,explanation:`Wish + past simple can describe an unwanted present or future situation: I wish I didn't have to get up early tomorrow.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'wish' the verb moves back one tense: don't have to becomes didn't have to.`,1:`We do not use 'would' after 'I wish I'.`,2:`The past perfect is for past regrets, but tomorrow is in the future.`},verb:`not have to`,options:[`don't have to`,`wouldn't have to`,`hadn't had to`,`didn't have to`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-033`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Do you ever wish you ___ in a different century?`,explanation:`Use wish + past perfect for something in the past that cannot change: I wish I had been born in the countryside.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Being born is a finished past event, so 'wish' needs the past perfect.`,1:`'would' does not work for a past event like birth.`,3:`The present simple is not used after 'wish'.`},verb:`be born`,options:[`were born`,`would be born`,`had been born`,`are born`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-034`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`We wish we ___ the house before prices went up.`,explanation:`Wish + past perfect expresses regret about the past: They wish they had sold their car earlier.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The regret is about the past, so we need the past perfect, not the past simple.`,2:`'would' is not used for things we regret not doing in the past.`,3:`The present perfect cannot follow 'wish'.`},verb:`buy`,options:[`had bought`,`bought`,`would buy`,`have bought`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-035`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`I wish it ___ so cold in here; can someone close the window?`,explanation:`Wish + past simple ('were' is common for 'be') describes an unreal present: I wish it weren't raining.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present simple is not used after 'wish'.`,2:`'would' is not normally used after 'wish' for states like being cold.`,3:`The past perfect is for the past, but the cold room is a present problem.`},verb:`not be`,options:[`weren't`,`isn't`,`wouldn't be`,`hadn't been`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-036`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`She wishes her boss ___ her more responsibility in the future.`,explanation:`Use wish + would when we want another person to act differently: I wish my brother would help more at home.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple is not used after 'wish'.`,1:`'will' does not follow 'wish'.`,2:`The present continuous is not used after 'wish'.`},verb:`give`,options:[`gives`,`will give`,`is giving`,`would give`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-037`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`If only I ___ my passport at home — now we can't check in!`,explanation:`'If only' + past perfect is used for a past mistake we regret: If only I hadn't lost my keys!`,wrongReasons:{0:`Leaving the passport happened before now, so the past perfect is needed.`,1:`'would' is for annoying habits, not a single past mistake.`,3:`The present perfect does not follow 'if only'.`},verb:`not leave`,options:[`didn't leave`,`wouldn't leave`,`hadn't left`,`haven't left`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-038`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`I wish I ___ what to say to make her feel better.`,explanation:`Wish + past simple describes an unreal present: I wish I knew her phone number.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'wish' a present situation needs the past simple.`,1:`We do not use 'would' after 'I wish I' for a present state.`,3:`The present perfect is not used after 'wish'.`},verb:`know`,options:[`know`,`would know`,`knew`,`have known`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-039`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`The children wish their parents ___ them stay up later.`,explanation:`Use wish + would + verb to show we want someone else to change: I wish my teacher would give us less homework.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'will' is never used after 'wish'.`,2:`'lets' is the present simple, which does not follow 'wish', and it does not agree with 'parents'.`,3:`The present continuous does not follow 'wish'.`},verb:`let`,options:[`would let`,`will let`,`lets`,`are letting`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-040`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`If only we ___ more time; our flight leaves in an hour.`,explanation:`'If only' + past simple expresses a strong wish about the present: If only I had more money.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple is not used after 'if only'.`,1:`'will' does not follow 'if only'.`,2:`'would' is not used after 'if only we' for a present state.`},verb:`have`,options:[`have`,`will have`,`would have`,`had`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-041`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`He wishes he ___ that rude comment to his colleague yesterday.`,explanation:`Wish + past perfect expresses regret for a past action: I wish I hadn't shouted at him.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'yesterday' shows a past regret, so the past perfect is needed.`,1:`'would' is not used for regrets about the past.`,3:`The present simple is not used after 'wish'.`},verb:`not make`,options:[`didn't make`,`wouldn't make`,`hadn't made`,`doesn't make`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-042`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`I wish you ___ me earlier that the concert had been cancelled.`,explanation:`Use wish + past perfect when you are sorry something did not happen: I wish you had invited me.`,wrongReasons:{1:`Telling happened in the past, so the regret needs the past perfect.`,2:`'would' is for present annoying habits, not past events.`,3:`The present perfect is not used after 'wish'.`},verb:`tell`,options:[`had told`,`told`,`would tell`,`have told`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-043`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`wish`,prompt:`Sometimes I wish I ___ a bit taller so I could reach the top shelf.`,explanation:`Wish + past simple ('were' for 'be') describes an unreal present: I wish I were better at maths.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple is not used after 'wish'.`,2:`'would' is not used after 'I wish I' for a state.`,3:`The past perfect is for the past, but my height is a present fact.`},verb:`be`,options:[`am`,`were`,`would be`,`had been`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-044`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`She said that she ___ the report the previous day.`,explanation:`In reported speech, a past action usually moves back to the past perfect. 'I finished it yesterday' → She said she had finished it the day before.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'the previous day' shows reported speech, so the present perfect moves back to the past perfect.`,1:`The present simple does not fit an action that was completed the day before.`,2:`'will finish' is about the future, but the action happened the previous day.`},verb:`finish`,options:[`has finished`,`finishes`,`will finish`,`had finished`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-045`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`He told me he ___ me the next week.`,explanation:`In reported speech 'will' usually becomes 'would'. 'I'll help you' → He said he would help me.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After a past reporting verb with 'the next week', 'will' changes to 'would'.`,1:`The present simple does not show the future promise from the past.`,2:`The present perfect is about something already done, not a promise.`},verb:`call`,options:[`will call`,`calls`,`has called`,`would call`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-046`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`The guide warned us ___ the animals.`,explanation:`Reported orders and warnings use verb + object + (not) to + verb: She told me not to worry.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'warn' is followed by object + (not) to + verb, not an imperative.`,1:`'warn somebody' needs the infinitive with 'to', not the -ing form.`,2:`After 'to' we need the base form, and 'not' normally goes before 'to'.`},verb:`not feed`,options:[`don't feed`,`not feeding`,`to not feeding`,`not to feed`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-047`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`My manager asked me whether I ___ the client yet.`,explanation:`Reported questions use statement word order and move tenses back. 'Have you called him?' → She asked if I had called him.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After the past verb 'asked', the present perfect moves back to the past perfect.`,2:`Reported questions use normal statement order, not an auxiliary like 'did'.`,3:`'contacts' does not agree with 'I' and does not fit 'yet'.`},verb:`contact`,options:[`have contacted`,`had contacted`,`did contact`,`contacts`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-048`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Maria asked me where I ___ my holiday the summer before.`,explanation:`In reported wh-questions, keep statement order and move the tense back: 'Where did you go?' → He asked where I had gone.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Reported questions do not use question word order or 'did'.`,2:`'the summer before' shows the tense must move back to the past perfect.`,3:`The present simple does not fit a holiday in the past.`},verb:`spend`,options:[`did I spend`,`had spent`,`have spent`,`spend`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-049`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`The teacher told the students ___ their phones during the exam.`,explanation:`Reported commands: tell + object + to + verb. 'Sit down!' → She told us to sit down.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'tell somebody' is followed by 'to + verb' in reported orders.`,1:`After 'told + object' we use the infinitive, not the -ing form.`,3:`'told them that they switch off' is not correct; for an order use 'told them to switch off'.`},verb:`switch off`,options:[`switch off`,`switching off`,`to switch off`,`that they switch off`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-050`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`He admitted ___ the money from the drawer.`,explanation:`Reporting verbs like admit, deny and suggest are followed by -ing: She admitted breaking the vase.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'admit' is followed by the -ing form, not the infinitive.`,1:`After 'admit' the base form alone is not possible.`,2:`'admitted took' puts two finite verbs together without a link.`},verb:`take`,options:[`to take`,`take`,`took`,`taking`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-051`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`She suggested ___ a taxi because it was getting late.`,explanation:`'suggest' + -ing reports a proposal: He suggested going to the beach.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'suggest' is never followed by the to-infinitive.`,2:`After 'suggest' the bare base form needs a subject, e.g. 'suggested we get'.`,3:`Two past verbs cannot follow each other like this without 'that' and a subject.`},verb:`get`,options:[`getting`,`to get`,`get`,`got`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-052`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`Jack said he ___ to Rome twice before.`,explanation:`The present perfect in direct speech becomes the past perfect: 'I've been there' → She said she had been there.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'before' and the past verb 'said' need the past perfect in reported speech.`,2:`'was to Rome' is not correct for experience; we say 'had been to'.`,3:`'would be' refers to the future, not a past experience.`},verb:`be`,options:[`had been`,`has been`,`was`,`would be`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-053`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`They asked us if we ___ help with the bags.`,explanation:`Reported yes/no questions: asked + if/whether + statement order, with the tense moved back. 'Are you hungry?' → She asked if I was hungry.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Reported yes/no questions use 'if' + statement order, without 'did'.`,1:`'needs' does not agree with 'we'.`,2:`'need' is a state verb and is not normally used in the continuous.`},verb:`need`,options:[`did we need`,`needs`,`were needing`,`needed`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-054`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`The doctor advised him ___ smoking immediately.`,explanation:`advise + object + to + verb: She advised me to see a lawyer.`,wrongReasons:{0:`With an object ('him'), 'advise' is followed by the to-infinitive.`,2:`'advise somebody' needs 'to' before the verb.`,3:`A second past verb cannot follow 'advised him' directly.`},verb:`give up`,options:[`giving up`,`to give up`,`give up`,`gave up`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-055`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`"I can't come tonight," Anna said. → Anna said she ___ come that night.`,explanation:`In reported speech 'can' usually becomes 'could' and 'can't' becomes 'couldn't': 'I can swim' → He said he could swim.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'could' changes the meaning: Anna said she can NOT come.`,1:`'couldn't have' is about the past, but she is talking about that night.`,2:`'can have' is not a correct way to report 'can't'.`},verb:`can`,options:[`could`,`couldn't have`,`can have`,`couldn't`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-056`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`The police officer asked the driver how fast he ___.`,explanation:`Reported questions keep statement word order: 'How fast were you going?' → She asked how fast he had been going.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Reported questions use statement order (he had), not question order.`,2:`After the past verb 'asked', the present perfect continuous moves back.`,3:`The present simple does not describe the driver's speed at that moment.`},verb:`go`,options:[`had he been going`,`had been going`,`has been going`,`goes`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-057`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`My sister reminded me ___ the plants while she was away.`,explanation:`remind + object + to + verb: He reminded me to buy milk.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'remind somebody' is followed by the to-infinitive.`,1:`After 'reminded me' we need 'to' before the verb.`,3:`'that' must be followed by a subject and a verb, e.g. 'that I should water'.`},verb:`water`,options:[`watering`,`water`,`to water`,`that water`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-058`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`He denied ___ the window, but we all saw him do it.`,explanation:`'deny' + -ing reports that someone says they did not do something: She denied stealing the phone.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'deny' takes the -ing form, not the infinitive.`,1:`The bare base form cannot follow 'deny'.`,3:`'that' needs a subject: 'denied that he broke'.`},verb:`break`,options:[`to break`,`break`,`breaking`,`that broke`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-059`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`"We are moving to Canada," they told us. → They told us they ___ to Canada.`,explanation:`The present continuous in direct speech becomes the past continuous: 'I'm working' → He said he was working.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'had moved' means the move was finished, but they were still planning it.`,2:`'would have moved' is a conditional form and changes the meaning.`,3:`'moves' does not agree with 'they' and does not move the tense back.`},verb:`move`,options:[`had moved`,`were moving`,`would have moved`,`moves`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-060`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`She asked me ___ her with her suitcase.`,explanation:`Reported requests: ask + object + to + verb. 'Can you open the door?' → She asked me to open the door.`,wrongReasons:{0:`When 'ask' means request, use object + to-infinitive, not -ing.`,1:`After 'asked me' we need 'to' before the verb.`,2:`'asked me that I help' is not correct; use 'asked me to help'.`},verb:`help`,options:[`helping`,`help`,`that I help`,`to help`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-061`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`The spokesperson explained that the flight ___ because of a technical problem earlier that morning.`,explanation:`Reported speech moves the tense back, and the passive keeps 'been': 'It has been delayed' → They said it had been delayed.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'earlier that morning' and the past verb 'explained' need the past perfect.`,1:`The flight did not cancel anything; it needs the passive form.`,3:`The past continuous suggests an action in progress, which does not fit here.`},verb:`cancel`,options:[`has been cancelled`,`had cancelled`,`had been cancelled`,`was cancelling`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-062`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`He apologised for ___ late to the meeting.`,explanation:`apologise for + -ing: She apologised for forgetting my birthday.`,wrongReasons:{1:`After the preposition 'for' we use the -ing form.`,2:`A preposition cannot be followed by the base form.`,3:`A past form cannot follow the preposition 'for'.`},verb:`arrive`,options:[`arriving`,`to arrive`,`arrive`,`arrived`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-063`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`reported-speech`,prompt:`The receptionist asked us what time we ___ to check out the next morning.`,explanation:`Reported wh-questions use statement order and backshift: 'What time do you want breakfast?' → She asked what time we wanted breakfast.`,wrongReasons:{1:`Reported questions do not use question order or 'did'.`,2:`After the past verb 'asked', the present tense usually moves back.`,3:`'will' should change to 'would', and 'want' here is a present wish.`},verb:`want`,options:[`wanted`,`did we want`,`want`,`will want`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-064`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`The new bridge ___ by the end of next year.`,explanation:`Future perfect passive: will have been + past participle. The work will have been finished by May.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The bridge cannot complete anything itself; we need the passive.`,2:`The present simple does not fit 'by the end of next year'.`,3:`The present perfect is about now, not a future deadline.`},verb:`complete`,options:[`will have been completed`,`will have completed`,`is completed`,`has been completed`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-065`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`My car ___ at the moment, so I'm taking the bus.`,explanation:`Present continuous passive: am/is/are being + past participle. The road is being cleaned now.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The car does not repair anything; use the passive.`,2:`This is active and means the car repaired something.`,3:`'at the moment' needs a present form, not the past.`},verb:`repair`,options:[`is being repaired`,`is repairing`,`has repaired`,`was repaired`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-066`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`The suspect ___ to have left the country last night.`,explanation:`Impersonal passive: subject + is believed/said/thought + to have + past participle. She is thought to have moved abroad.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The suspect is not the one who believes; people believe this about him.`,1:`'believe' is a state verb and is not normally used in the continuous.`,2:`The active present perfect gives the wrong meaning.`},verb:`believe`,options:[`believes`,`is believing`,`has believed`,`is believed`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-067`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`I had my hair ___ yesterday.`,explanation:`have/get + object + past participle means someone does it for us: I had my car washed.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'have something done' needs the past participle, not the -ing form.`,2:`'have something done' does not use the to-infinitive.`,3:`The present simple cannot follow 'had my hair'.`},verb:`cut`,options:[`cut`,`cutting`,`to cut`,`cuts`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-068`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`The castle ___ in the twelfth century and is now a museum.`,explanation:`Past simple passive: was/were + past participle, often with a past time. The church was built in 1850.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The castle did not build anything; we need the passive.`,1:`The present perfect does not go with a finished past time like 'the twelfth century'.`,3:`This is active and the past perfect is not needed.`},verb:`build`,options:[`built`,`has been built`,`was built`,`had built`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-069`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`All the tickets ___ by the time we got to the box office.`,explanation:`Past perfect passive: had been + past participle, for something done before another past event. The room had been cleaned before we arrived.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The story is in the past, so we need the past perfect, not the present perfect.`,1:`The past continuous does not show that the sale was finished.`,2:`The present simple does not fit a past story.`},verb:`sell`,options:[`have been sold`,`were selling`,`are sold`,`had been sold`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-070`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Our house ___ when we arrived, so the workers asked us to wait outside.`,explanation:`Past continuous passive: was/were being + past participle, for something in progress in the past. The road was being repaired when we drove past.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The house cannot paint; the passive is needed.`,2:`The story is in the past, so the present continuous does not fit.`,3:`This is active and in the wrong tense.`},verb:`paint`,options:[`was being painted`,`was painting`,`is being painted`,`has painted`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-071`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`It ___ that the new law will come into force in January.`,explanation:`Impersonal passive: It is expected/said/believed that... It is said that the house is haunted.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'It expects' is active and makes no sense here; use the impersonal passive.`,2:`The continuous active form does not express a general belief.`,3:`The active present perfect is not used in this structure.`},verb:`expect`,options:[`is expected`,`expects`,`is expecting`,`has expected`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-072`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`This report must ___ before Friday.`,explanation:`Modal passive: modal + be + past participle. The form should be signed today.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The report cannot send anything; after a modal use 'be + past participle'.`,2:`After 'must' we need the base form 'be', not 'been'.`,3:`A modal is not followed by the -ing form.`},verb:`send`,options:[`be sent`,`send`,`been sent`,`being sent`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-073`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`The thief ___ by a security guard as he was leaving the shop.`,explanation:`Past simple passive with 'by' shows who did the action: The window was broken by a ball.`,wrongReasons:{0:`With 'by a security guard', the thief receives the action, so we need the passive.`,1:`The present perfect does not fit a story with 'as he was leaving'.`,3:`The thief was not catching anyone; the passive is needed.`},verb:`catch`,options:[`caught`,`has been caught`,`was caught`,`was catching`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-074`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Thousands of new trees ___ in the park since last spring.`,explanation:`Present perfect passive: have/has been + past participle, often with since/for. Many houses have been built since 2020.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'since last spring' needs the present perfect, not the past simple.`,2:`Trees cannot plant; use the passive.`,3:`The present simple does not go with 'since'.`},verb:`plant`,options:[`were planted`,`have been planted`,`have planted`,`are planted`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-075`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`I don't like ___ what to do by people I hardly know.`,explanation:`Passive -ing form: being + past participle. She hates being interrupted.`,wrongReasons:{0:`With 'by people', I receive the action, so we need the passive -ing form.`,1:`After 'like' the bare form 'be told' is not correct.`,3:`'like' cannot be followed directly by a past participle.`},verb:`tell`,options:[`telling`,`be told`,`being told`,`told`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-076`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`The results ___ online next Monday.`,explanation:`Future passive: will be + past participle. The winners will be announced tomorrow.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The results cannot publish anything; use the passive.`,2:`This is active and suggests the results are doing the action.`,3:`'next Monday' is in the future, so the present perfect does not fit.`},verb:`publish`,options:[`will publish`,`will be published`,`are publishing`,`have been published`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-077`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Smoking ___ in all restaurants in the country since 2010.`,explanation:`Present perfect passive with 'since': has/have been + past participle. The road has been closed since Monday.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'since 2010' needs the present perfect, not the past simple.`,2:`The present simple does not go with 'since'.`,3:`Smoking did not ban anything; use the passive.`},verb:`ban`,options:[`was banned`,`has been banned`,`is banned`,`has banned`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-078`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`She's getting her new kitchen ___ next week.`,explanation:`get + object + past participle means we pay someone to do it: We're getting the roof repaired.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'get something done' needs the past participle, not the base form.`,1:`'get + object + -ing' has a different meaning; here someone does the job for her.`,3:`'get something done' does not use the to-infinitive.`},verb:`install`,options:[`install`,`installing`,`installed`,`to install`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-079`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`The patient ___ by three different doctors before anyone found the problem.`,explanation:`Past perfect passive shows an earlier past action done to the subject: The letter had been opened before I got it.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The patient did not examine anyone; use the passive.`,1:`This is active and in the wrong tense.`,2:`The story is in the past ('found'), so use the past perfect.`},verb:`examine`,options:[`had examined`,`was examining`,`has been examined`,`had been examined`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-080`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`He ___ to be the richest man in the village.`,explanation:`Impersonal passive: subject + is said/believed + to + verb. She is said to be very kind.`,wrongReasons:{0:`He is not the speaker; other people say this about him.`,2:`The continuous active form does not express general opinion.`,3:`The active present perfect changes the meaning completely.`},verb:`say`,options:[`says`,`is said`,`is saying`,`has said`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-081`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`The windows need ___; they're really dirty.`,explanation:`need + -ing has a passive meaning: The car needs washing (= needs to be washed).`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'need' we cannot use the bare base form.`,1:`'need' is not followed directly by a past participle in standard English.`,2:`'The windows need to clean' means the windows will do the cleaning.`},verb:`clean`,options:[`clean`,`cleaned`,`to clean`,`cleaning`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-082`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Hundreds of emails ___ every day by our customer service team.`,explanation:`Present simple passive for regular actions: am/is/are + past participle. The office is cleaned every evening.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The emails do not answer anything; we need the passive.`,2:`This is active and suggests the emails are writing replies.`,3:`The active present perfect does not fit 'every day'.`},verb:`answer`,options:[`are answered`,`answer`,`are answering`,`have answered`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-083`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`The novel ___ into more than forty languages so far.`,explanation:`Present perfect passive with 'so far': has/have been + past participle. Two million copies have been sold so far.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'so far' needs the present perfect, not the past simple.`,2:`A novel cannot translate; use the passive.`,3:`This is active and suggests the novel is doing the work.`},verb:`translate`,options:[`was translated`,`has been translated`,`has translated`,`is translating`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-084`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`You ___ tired after such a long flight. Sit down and relax.`,explanation:`'must + verb' shows we are almost sure something is true now: She must be at home; her car is outside.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'can't be' means it is impossible, but after a long flight tiredness is very likely.`,1:`'must have been' is about the past, but the person is tired now.`,3:`'mustn't' expresses prohibition, not deduction.`},verb:`be`,options:[`can't be`,`must have been`,`must be`,`mustn't be`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-085`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`She ___ the message; otherwise she would have replied by now.`,explanation:`can't have + past participle shows we are sure something did not happen: He can't have seen me; he didn't wave.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'mustn't have' is not used for negative deductions; we say 'can't have'.`,2:`'can't read' means she is not able to read, which is not the point.`,3:`'shouldn't have' criticises a past action, it is not a deduction.`},verb:`read`,options:[`mustn't have read`,`can't have read`,`can't read`,`shouldn't have read`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-086`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`The lights are off. They ___ to bed already.`,explanation:`must have + past participle shows we are almost sure about the past: The ground is wet; it must have rained.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'must go' is an obligation or a present deduction, not a deduction about a past action.`,1:`The lights are off, so it is likely, not impossible, that they went to bed.`,3:`'should go' is advice, not a deduction about the past.`},verb:`go`,options:[`must go`,`can't have gone`,`must have gone`,`should go`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-087`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`You ___ a ticket for the museum; entry is free for students.`,explanation:`'don't have to' means it is not necessary: You don't have to come, but you can if you want.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'mustn't' means it is forbidden, but buying a ticket is allowed, just not necessary.`,1:`'must buy' says it is necessary, but entry is free.`,3:`'can't buy' means it is impossible, which is not the meaning here.`},verb:`buy`,options:[`mustn't buy`,`must buy`,`don't have to buy`,`can't buy`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-088`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`You ___ me you were coming! I would have cooked dinner.`,explanation:`should have + past participle criticises something that did not happen: You should have called me.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The visit has already happened, so we need 'should have + past participle'.`,2:`'must have told' is a deduction, but the speaker knows they were not told.`,3:`'can't have told' is a deduction, not a complaint about the past.`},verb:`tell`,options:[`should tell`,`should have told`,`must have told`,`can't have told`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-089`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`I'm not sure where Lisa is. She ___ in the library, or maybe she's gone home.`,explanation:`might/may/could + verb show a present possibility: He might be in the kitchen.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'must' shows certainty, but the speaker is not sure.`,1:`'can't be' says it is impossible, but the library is one possibility.`,3:`'mustn't' expresses prohibition, not possibility.`},verb:`be`,options:[`must be`,`can't be`,`might be`,`mustn't be`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-090`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`That ___ Paul you saw at the station; he's on holiday in Spain this week.`,explanation:`can't have been shows something in the past was impossible: It can't have been Anna; she was with me.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'must have been' shows certainty that it was Paul, but he is in Spain.`,2:`'mustn't have' is not used for deductions about the past.`,3:`'can't be' is about now, but the sighting was in the past.`},verb:`be`,options:[`must have been`,`can't have been`,`mustn't have been`,`can't be`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-091`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`We ___; the train doesn't leave for another hour.`,explanation:`'don't need to' means something is not necessary: You don't need to bring food; there's a café.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'mustn't' means hurrying is forbidden, but it is only unnecessary.`,1:`'needn't have hurried' is about the past, but we are talking about now.`,2:`'need to hurry' means it is necessary, which contradicts the extra hour.`},verb:`hurry`,options:[`mustn't hurry`,`needn't have hurried`,`need to hurry`,`don't need to hurry`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-092`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`When I was younger, I ___ run ten kilometres without stopping.`,explanation:`'could' expresses general ability in the past: My grandmother could speak four languages.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'can' is present, but 'when I was younger' is in the past.`,2:`'could have' is for something possible in the past that did not happen.`,3:`The present perfect does not go with 'when I was younger'.`},verb:`can`,options:[`could`,`can`,`could have`,`have been able to`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-093`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Despite the storm, the pilot ___ land the plane safely.`,explanation:`Use 'was/were able to' for one particular achievement in the past: After hours of trying, we were able to open the door.`,wrongReasons:{0:`For one specific success in the past, we use 'was able to' or 'managed to', not 'could'.`,2:`'can' is present, but the landing happened in the past.`,3:`The present perfect does not fit a single finished event in the past.`},verb:`be able to`,options:[`could`,`was able to`,`can`,`has been able to`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-094`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`You ___ wear a helmet when you ride a motorbike; it's the law.`,explanation:`'have to' expresses an obligation, often from a rule or law: You have to show your passport at the border.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'don't have to' means it is not necessary, but the law requires it.`,2:`'had to' is the past, but this is a general rule now.`,3:`The -ing form cannot be the main verb after 'you'.`},verb:`have to`,options:[`don't have to`,`have to`,`had to`,`having to`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-095`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`You ___ your phone during the exam; it's strictly forbidden.`,explanation:`'mustn't' expresses prohibition: You mustn't park here.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'don't have to' means it is optional, but here it is forbidden.`,2:`'must use' is an obligation, the opposite of the meaning.`,3:`'needn't' means it is unnecessary, not forbidden.`},verb:`use`,options:[`don't have to use`,`mustn't use`,`must use`,`needn't use`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-096`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`We ___ a taxi; the hotel was only five minutes' walk away, so it was a waste of money.`,explanation:`needn't have + past participle means we did something that was not necessary: You needn't have brought a gift.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'mustn't have' is not used to talk about unnecessary past actions.`,2:`'needn't take' is about the present or future, but we took the taxi in the past.`,3:`'can't have taken' is a deduction, but we know we took a taxi.`},verb:`take`,options:[`mustn't have taken`,`needn't have taken`,`needn't take`,`can't have taken`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-097`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Someone ___ the door open; the cat got out during the night.`,explanation:`must have + past participle is a confident guess about the past: She must have forgotten our meeting.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'must leave' is an obligation about now, not a deduction about the night.`,1:`The cat got out, so it is very likely, not impossible, that the door was open.`,3:`'should leave' gives advice, which does not fit.`},verb:`leave`,options:[`must leave`,`can't have left`,`must have left`,`should leave`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-098`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`He ___ at work today; I saw him at the gym ten minutes ago.`,explanation:`can't + verb shows we are sure something is not true now: That can't be the right answer; it's too high.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'must be' shows we think it is true, but the gym sighting shows the opposite.`,1:`'mustn't' is for prohibition, not negative deduction.`,3:`'needn't' is about necessity, not about what is logically possible.`},verb:`be`,options:[`must be`,`mustn't be`,`can't be`,`needn't be`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-099`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`If you feel ill, you ___ a doctor as soon as possible.`,explanation:`'should + verb' gives advice: You should drink more water.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'should have seen' criticises the past, but this is advice for now.`,2:`'mustn't' forbids the action, the opposite of the advice.`,3:`'needn't' says it is unnecessary, which contradicts 'as soon as possible'.`},verb:`see`,options:[`should see`,`should have seen`,`mustn't see`,`needn't see`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-100`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Take an umbrella; it ___ later this afternoon.`,explanation:`might/may + verb shows a future possibility: I might go to the party on Saturday.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'must' shows certainty or obligation, but rain is only possible.`,2:`'might have rained' is about the past, but the afternoon is in the future.`,3:`'can't rain' says rain is impossible, so an umbrella would not be needed.`},verb:`rain`,options:[`must rain`,`might rain`,`might have rained`,`can't rain`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-101`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Jane looks delighted. She ___ her driving test.`,explanation:`must have + past participle: a strong deduction about the past from present evidence. He looks tired; he must have worked late.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'must pass' expresses obligation, not a deduction about the past.`,1:`Her happy face suggests the opposite.`,2:`'mustn't have' is not used for deductions.`},verb:`pass`,options:[`must pass`,`can't have passed`,`mustn't have passed`,`must have passed`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-102`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`The car broke down, so we ___ walk the last five miles.`,explanation:`'had to' is the past of both 'must' and 'have to': We had to wait for an hour.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'have to' is present, but the breakdown was in the past.`,1:`The present perfect does not fit a finished past story.`,3:`The past perfect is not needed; this is the main event of the story.`},verb:`have to`,options:[`have to`,`have had to`,`had to`,`had had to`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-103`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`You ___ worry about the dog; my neighbour will feed it while we're away.`,explanation:`'needn't + verb' means it is not necessary: You needn't wait for me.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Positive 'need' as a modal is not used like this; the meaning is 'it is not necessary'.`,2:`'needn't have' needs a past participle and refers to the past.`,3:`The past form does not fit advice about now.`},verb:`need`,options:[`need`,`needn't`,`needn't have`,`needed`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-104`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`While I ___ dinner, the power suddenly went off.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for a longer action interrupted by a shorter one: While I was reading, the phone rang.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The past simple does not show the longer action that was interrupted.`,2:`The past perfect means dinner was finished before, but 'while' shows it was in progress.`,3:`The present continuous does not fit a story in the past.`},verb:`cook`,options:[`was cooking`,`cooked`,`had cooked`,`am cooking`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-105`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`She ___ for three hours when the rescue team finally found her.`,explanation:`Past perfect continuous: had been + -ing, for an action that continued up to a past moment. They had been walking for hours when it got dark.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'for three hours' before another past event needs the past perfect continuous.`,1:`The present perfect continuous connects to now, but this story is in the past.`,2:`The past simple does not show that the waiting continued up to the moment she was found.`},verb:`wait`,options:[`was waiting`,`has been waiting`,`waited`,`had been waiting`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-106`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`He couldn't get into the flat because he ___ his keys at the office.`,explanation:`Use the past perfect for an action that happened before another past moment: I couldn't pay because I had forgotten my wallet.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present perfect does not fit a story in the past.`,2:`The past continuous suggests an action in progress, not a finished earlier action.`,3:`The present simple does not fit a past situation.`},verb:`leave`,options:[`has left`,`had left`,`was leaving`,`leaves`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-107`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`The phone rang just as I ___ out of the door.`,explanation:`The past continuous shows an action in progress when something else happened: I was getting into the car when I heard a noise.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present perfect does not fit a past story.`,1:`The present continuous does not match the past verb 'rang'.`,3:`'walks' does not agree with 'I' and is not a past form.`},verb:`walk`,options:[`have walked`,`am walking`,`was walking`,`walks`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-108`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`It ___ all night, and the streets were still wet in the morning.`,explanation:`The past perfect continuous explains a past result: The grass was wet because it had been raining.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The story is in the past ('were'), so the present perfect continuous does not fit.`,2:`'rain' cannot be passive.`,3:`The present simple does not fit a past story.`},verb:`rain`,options:[`had been raining`,`has been raining`,`was rained`,`rains`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-109`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`When I arrived at the party, most of the guests ___ home.`,explanation:`Use the past perfect for an action finished before another past action: When we got to the station, the train had left.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present perfect does not fit a past story.`,2:`'goes' does not agree with 'guests' and is not a past form.`,3:`This form is present and singular, so it fits neither the time nor the subject.`},verb:`go`,options:[`have gone`,`had gone`,`goes`,`has been going`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-110`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`We ___ along the beach when we saw a dolphin jump out of the water.`,explanation:`The past continuous sets the background scene in a story: We were driving home when we saw the accident.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present perfect does not fit a story with 'saw'.`,1:`The present simple does not fit a past story.`,3:`The present continuous does not match the past verb 'saw'.`},verb:`walk`,options:[`have walked`,`walk`,`were walking`,`are walking`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-111`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`By the time the firefighters arrived, the fire ___ most of the building.`,explanation:`'By the time' + past simple is usually followed by the past perfect: By the time I woke up, everyone had left.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'By the time' shows the damage was already complete, so the past perfect is needed.`,1:`The present perfect does not fit a past story.`,2:`The fire did the destroying, so the passive is wrong here.`},verb:`destroy`,options:[`destroyed`,`has destroyed`,`was destroyed`,`had destroyed`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-112`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`He was exhausted because he ___ in the garden all afternoon.`,explanation:`The past perfect continuous explains why someone felt a certain way in the past: Her hands were dirty because she had been painting.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present perfect continuous does not match 'was exhausted'.`,2:`The present simple does not explain a past situation.`,3:`The present continuous does not fit a past story.`},verb:`work`,options:[`had been working`,`has been working`,`works`,`is working`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-113`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`I recognised the woman immediately; I ___ her before at a conference.`,explanation:`The past perfect shows an earlier past experience: I knew the city well because I had lived there.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present perfect does not fit after the past verb 'recognised'.`,1:`The past continuous does not show a completed earlier event.`,3:`The present simple does not fit a past event.`},verb:`meet`,options:[`have met`,`was meeting`,`had met`,`meet`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-114`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`The children ___ football in the garden when it started to hail.`,explanation:`Past continuous for the interrupted activity, past simple for the interruption: We were having lunch when the guests arrived.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The past simple does not show the background activity that was interrupted.`,2:`The present perfect does not fit a past story.`,3:`This is passive, but the children were doing the action.`},verb:`play`,options:[`played`,`were playing`,`have played`,`had been played`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-115`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`The thief ___ through the back window while the family were having dinner.`,explanation:`Use the past simple for a short completed action during a longer one: Someone knocked while I was sleeping.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present perfect does not fit a past story.`,2:`This is passive, but the thief did the action.`,3:`The present simple does not fit a past story.`},verb:`climb`,options:[`climbed`,`has climbed`,`had been climbed`,`climbs`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-116`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Sarah ___ in Paris for ten years before she moved to London.`,explanation:`Use the past perfect for a period before another past event: He had worked there for five years before he left.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present perfect does not fit a story about the past.`,1:`The present continuous does not fit a past period.`,3:`The present simple does not fit a past period.`},verb:`live`,options:[`has lived`,`is living`,`had lived`,`lives`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-117`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Her eyes were red because she ___.`,explanation:`The past perfect continuous explains visible results in the past: He was out of breath because he had been running.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present perfect continuous does not match 'were red'.`,2:`The present simple does not explain a past situation.`,3:`The present continuous does not fit a past description.`},verb:`cry`,options:[`had been crying`,`has been crying`,`cries`,`is crying`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-118`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`We ___ for about an hour when the car broke down.`,explanation:`Past perfect continuous with 'for' shows how long something continued before a past event: I had been studying for two hours when you called.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present perfect continuous does not fit a story with 'broke down'.`,1:`The past simple does not show that the driving continued for an hour up to the breakdown.`,2:`The present continuous does not fit a past story.`},verb:`drive`,options:[`have been driving`,`drove`,`are driving`,`had been driving`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-119`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`I didn't go to the cinema with them because I ___ the film already.`,explanation:`Use the past perfect for an earlier experience in a past story: She didn't want to eat because she had already had lunch.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present perfect does not fit after 'didn't go'.`,2:`The past continuous does not show a completed earlier experience.`,3:`The present simple does not fit a past reason.`},verb:`see`,options:[`had seen`,`have seen`,`was seeing`,`see`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-120`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`While my parents ___ abroad, I looked after their house.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous with 'while' for a longer background activity: While she was studying abroad, she learned Spanish.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present perfect does not fit a past story.`,1:`The present continuous does not match 'looked after'.`,3:`This is passive, but the parents did the travelling.`},verb:`travel`,options:[`have travelled`,`are travelling`,`were travelling`,`had been travelled`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-121`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`Most people in the town ___ when the earthquake hit at three in the morning.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for an action in progress at a past moment: At midnight I was still working.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The past simple suggests they went to sleep after the earthquake, not that they were asleep.`,2:`The present perfect does not fit a past event.`,3:`The present simple does not fit a past story.`},verb:`sleep`,options:[`slept`,`were sleeping`,`have slept`,`sleep`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-122`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`When the police arrived, the robbers ___ — there was nobody left in the bank.`,explanation:`The past perfect shows that one action was finished before another past action: When I got home, the guests had left.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present perfect does not fit a past story.`,2:`'escape' is not used in the passive like this.`,3:`The present simple does not fit a past story.`},verb:`escape`,options:[`have escaped`,`had escaped`,`were escaped`,`escape`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-123`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`I ___ a shower when the doorbell rang, so I didn't hear it at first.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for the activity in progress when something interrupted it: I was washing up when the lights went out.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The past simple suggests the shower started after the doorbell rang.`,1:`The present perfect does not fit a past story.`,2:`The present continuous does not match 'rang'.`},verb:`have`,options:[`had`,`have had`,`am having`,`was having`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-124`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`narrative-tenses`,prompt:`He ___ for the company for only two months when he was promoted.`,explanation:`Use the past perfect continuous to say how long something had continued before a past event: She had been teaching for a year when she got the award.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present perfect continuous does not match 'was promoted'.`,2:`'work' is not passive here; he did the working.`,3:`The present simple does not fit a past story.`},verb:`work`,options:[`has been working`,`had been working`,`was worked`,`works`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-125`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Look at those dark clouds! It ___.`,explanation:`Use 'going to' for predictions based on what we can see now: Be careful, you're going to fall!`,wrongReasons:{0:`The past simple does not fit a prediction about what will happen soon.`,2:`The present perfect talks about something already finished, not a prediction.`,3:`The present simple is for habits and facts, not predictions based on evidence.`},verb:`rain`,options:[`rained`,`is going to rain`,`has rained`,`rains`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-126`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`This time next week, I ___ on a beach in Greece.`,explanation:`Future continuous: will be + -ing, for an action in progress at a future time. At 8 p.m. tomorrow, I'll be watching the match.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'This time next week' needs an action in progress, so use the future continuous.`,2:`The present simple does not fit an action in progress in the future.`,3:`The future perfect shows completion, not an ongoing activity.`},verb:`lie`,options:[`will be lying`,`will lie`,`lie`,`will have lain`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-127`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`According to the timetable, the next train ___ at 7.45.`,explanation:`Use the present simple for timetables and schedules: The film starts at 9 p.m.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The past simple refers to a departure that has already happened.`,1:`The present perfect says the train is already gone, not when it departs.`,2:`The past perfect does not fit a timetable.`},verb:`leave`,options:[`left`,`has left`,`had left`,`leaves`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-128`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`By the time you get home, I ___ dinner.`,explanation:`Future perfect: will have + past participle, for actions completed before a future time. By Friday, I will have finished the project.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'By the time' shows the action will be complete before then, so use the future perfect.`,2:`The present simple does not show completion before a future moment.`,3:`The present continuous does not show the action will be finished.`},verb:`make`,options:[`will make`,`will have made`,`make`,`am making`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-129`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`I ___ my grandparents this weekend — I've already bought the train tickets.`,explanation:`Use the present continuous for fixed future arrangements: We're meeting Tom on Saturday.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present perfect says the visit is already over, but it is this weekend.`,2:`The present simple is for timetables, not personal arrangements.`,3:`The past simple does not fit 'this weekend' in the future.`},verb:`visit`,options:[`am visiting`,`have visited`,`visit`,`visited`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-130`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Don't worry about the heavy box — I ___ you carry it.`,explanation:`Use 'will' for offers and decisions made at the moment of speaking: I'll open the door for you.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present continuous is for arrangements, not offers made at the moment of speaking.`,2:`The present simple is not used for spontaneous offers.`,3:`The past simple does not fit an offer about the next moment.`},verb:`help`,options:[`am helping`,`'ll help`,`help`,`helped`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-131`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`By next June, they ___ married for twenty-five years.`,explanation:`Future perfect with 'for' shows the length of time up to a future point: By May, I will have lived here for ten years.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'By next June' plus a period of time needs the future perfect.`,2:`The present simple does not fit a future point in time.`,3:`The present perfect does not reach to a future time like 'next June'.`},verb:`be`,options:[`will be`,`will have been`,`are`,`have been`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-132`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Don't call me at nine; I ___ my weekly yoga class then.`,explanation:`The future continuous describes what will be happening at a future moment: Don't come at six; I'll be cooking.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The future perfect means the class will be over, but it is in progress at nine.`,2:`The present perfect does not refer to a future time.`,3:`The past simple does not fit a future time.`},verb:`do`,options:[`will be doing`,`will have done`,`have done`,`did`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-133`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`When I ___ my exams, I'll go on holiday.`,explanation:`After when, as soon as, before and after, use a present tense for the future: I'll call you when I arrive.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After time words like 'when' we use a present tense, not 'will', for the future.`,1:`'would' does not fit this real future plan.`,3:`The past simple does not fit a future plan with 'I'll'.`},verb:`finish`,options:[`will finish`,`would finish`,`finish`,`finished`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-134`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`She ___ her thesis by the end of the month, if all goes well.`,explanation:`Use the future perfect with 'by + time': I will have read the book by Monday.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present simple does not show completion before a future deadline.`,2:`The present perfect refers to now, not to a future deadline.`,3:`The past simple does not fit a future deadline.`},verb:`complete`,options:[`will have completed`,`completes`,`has completed`,`completed`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-135`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`I think robots ___ most of our housework in the future.`,explanation:`Use 'will' for predictions based on opinion, often after 'I think': I think it will be a great year.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present continuous is for arrangements, not general predictions about the far future.`,1:`The past simple does not fit 'in the future'.`,2:`The present perfect does not fit a prediction about the future.`},verb:`do`,options:[`are doing`,`did`,`have done`,`will do`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-136`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`I've decided: I ___ for a new job next month.`,explanation:`Use 'going to' for intentions and plans already decided: I'm going to learn Japanese next year.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple is not used for personal plans.`,1:`The past simple does not fit 'next month'.`,3:`The present perfect says it is already done, not a plan.`},verb:`apply`,options:[`apply`,`applied`,`am going to apply`,`have applied`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-137`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Next year, my parents ___ for the same company for thirty years.`,explanation:`Future perfect with 'for': will have + past participle. In July, she will have taught here for twenty years.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The future simple does not show the length of time up to a future point.`,1:`The present simple does not show duration up to a future time.`,2:`The present continuous does not show thirty years of work completed by next year.`},verb:`work`,options:[`will work`,`work`,`are working`,`will have worked`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-138`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`"The phone's ringing." "I ___ it."`,explanation:`Use 'will' for instant decisions: "There's someone at the door." "I'll go."`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present simple is not used for a decision made at the moment of speaking.`,2:`The past simple does not fit an immediate decision.`,3:`The present perfect says it is already done.`},verb:`get`,options:[`'ll get`,`get`,`got`,`have got`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-139`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`As soon as the results ___, we will let you know.`,explanation:`After 'as soon as' use a present tense for the future: I'll text you as soon as I get there.`,wrongReasons:{1:`After 'as soon as' we use the present simple, not 'will', for the future.`,2:`'would' does not fit this real future situation.`,3:`The past simple does not match 'we will let you know'.`},verb:`arrive`,options:[`arrive`,`will arrive`,`would arrive`,`arrived`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-140`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Sorry, I can't come to dinner on Saturday; I ___ my cousin's wedding.`,explanation:`Use the present continuous for arrangements in your diary: I'm seeing the dentist on Monday.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple is not used for personal arrangements.`,2:`The past simple does not fit 'on Saturday' in the future.`,3:`The present perfect says the wedding is already over.`},verb:`attend`,options:[`attend`,`am attending`,`attended`,`have attended`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-141`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`If the trend continues, the population of the city ___ by 2040.`,explanation:`Use the future perfect for a change completed before a future date: Prices will have risen by next year.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple does not show a change completed by a future date.`,1:`The present perfect says it has already happened.`,2:`The present continuous does not go with 'by 2040'.`},verb:`double`,options:[`doubles`,`has doubled`,`is doubling`,`will have doubled`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-142`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Don't phone after ten; we ___ to bed by then.`,explanation:`'by then' often goes with the future perfect: By midnight, everyone will have left.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'by then' shows completion before a time, so use the future perfect.`,1:`The present simple does not show completion before a future time.`,3:`The present perfect refers to now, not to ten o'clock tonight.`},verb:`go`,options:[`'ll go`,`go`,`'ll have gone`,`have gone`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-143`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`I promise I ___ anyone your secret.`,explanation:`Use 'will/won't' for promises: I promise I won't be late.`,wrongReasons:{1:`Promises about the future use 'will', not the present simple.`,2:`The present perfect is about the past, but a promise is about the future.`,3:`The past continuous does not fit a promise.`},verb:`not tell`,options:[`won't tell`,`don't tell`,`haven't told`,`wasn't telling`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-144`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`I ___ you as soon as I land in Tokyo.`,explanation:`Use 'will' in the main clause and a present tense after 'as soon as': I'll call you as soon as I hear anything.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple is not used for a promise in the main clause.`,1:`The present continuous is for arrangements, not promises tied to 'as soon as'.`,2:`The past simple does not fit a future action.`},verb:`text`,options:[`text`,`am texting`,`texted`,`'ll text`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-145`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`At this time tomorrow, we ___ over the Atlantic.`,explanation:`Future continuous for an action in progress at a future moment: At noon tomorrow, I'll be sitting an exam.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The future perfect shows the flight finished, but we will be in the air.`,1:`The present simple does not show an action in progress.`,2:`The past simple does not fit 'tomorrow'.`},verb:`fly`,options:[`will have flown`,`fly`,`flew`,`will be flying`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-146`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`I'm really looking forward to ___ you next week.`,explanation:`'look forward to' + -ing: We're looking forward to meeting you.`,wrongReasons:{0:`In 'look forward to', 'to' is a preposition, so it needs the -ing form.`,1:`'look forward' is followed by 'to + -ing', not another 'to'.`,3:`A past form cannot follow the preposition 'to'.`},verb:`see`,options:[`see`,`to see`,`seeing`,`saw`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-147`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`She managed ___ the problem without any help.`,explanation:`'manage' + to-infinitive: He managed to fix the bike.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'manage' is followed by the to-infinitive, not -ing.`,1:`'manage' needs 'to' before the verb.`,2:`Two past verbs cannot follow each other directly.`},verb:`solve`,options:[`solving`,`solve`,`solved`,`to solve`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-148`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Would you mind ___ the window? It's freezing.`,explanation:`'Would you mind' + -ing is a polite request: Would you mind waiting a moment?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'mind' is followed by the -ing form, not the infinitive.`,2:`'mind' cannot be followed by the base form.`,3:`'Would you mind closed' is not grammatical.`},verb:`close`,options:[`to close`,`closing`,`close`,`closed`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-149`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`I'll never forget ___ the Northern Lights for the first time.`,explanation:`'forget + -ing' is about a past memory: I'll never forget meeting my favourite singer.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'forget to do' means not remembering to do something, not a memory of the past.`,2:`The base form cannot follow 'forget'.`,3:`A past form cannot follow 'forget' directly.`},verb:`see`,options:[`to see`,`seeing`,`see`,`saw`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-150`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Remember ___ the door when you leave.`,explanation:`'remember + to-infinitive' is about a future duty: Remember to call your mother.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'remember + -ing' is about a past memory, but this is something to do in the future.`,1:`'remember' needs 'to' before the verb here.`,2:`A past form cannot follow 'remember' in this instruction.`},verb:`lock`,options:[`locking`,`lock`,`locked`,`to lock`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-151`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`After driving for four hours, we stopped ___ something to eat.`,explanation:`'stop + to-infinitive' means stop in order to do something: She stopped to tie her shoe.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'stop + -ing' means to quit an activity; here we stopped in order to eat.`,1:`The base form cannot follow 'stopped' here.`,2:`A past form cannot follow 'stopped' directly.`},verb:`get`,options:[`getting`,`get`,`got`,`to get`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-152`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`My boss made me ___ the whole report again.`,explanation:`make + object + base form: The teacher made us repeat the exercise.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'make somebody' is followed by the base form without 'to' in the active.`,1:`'make somebody' is not followed by the -ing form.`,2:`'made me written' is not correct; the past participle does not fit here.`},verb:`write`,options:[`to write`,`writing`,`written`,`write`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-153`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`I can't afford ___ my laptop this year.`,explanation:`'can't afford' + to-infinitive: We can't afford to go on holiday.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'afford' takes the to-infinitive, not the -ing form.`,1:`'afford' needs 'to' before the verb.`,2:`A past form cannot follow 'afford'.`},verb:`replace`,options:[`replacing`,`replace`,`replaced`,`to replace`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-154`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`She avoided ___ eye contact with the interviewer.`,explanation:`'avoid' + -ing: He avoided answering the question.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'avoid' is followed by the -ing form, never the infinitive.`,2:`The base form cannot follow 'avoid'.`,3:`A past form cannot follow 'avoided'.`},verb:`make`,options:[`making`,`to make`,`make`,`made`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-155`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`I regret ___ you that your application has been unsuccessful.`,explanation:`'regret to + verb' is a formal way to give bad news: We regret to announce that the flight is cancelled.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'regret + -ing' means being sorry about a past action; formal bad news uses 'regret to'.`,2:`'regret' needs 'to' before the verb here.`,3:`A past form cannot follow 'regret' directly.`},verb:`inform`,options:[`to inform`,`informing`,`inform`,`informed`],correct:0},{id:`b2-vf-156`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`He's used to ___ up early because he's a baker.`,explanation:`'be used to + -ing' means something is normal for you: I'm used to working at night.`,wrongReasons:{0:`In 'be used to', 'to' is a preposition, so it needs -ing.`,1:`A past form cannot follow 'to' here.`,2:`'used to have got' is not correct after 'be used to'.`},verb:`get`,options:[`get`,`got`,`have got`,`getting`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-157`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`My parents let me ___ to the festival with my friends.`,explanation:`let + object + base form: My teacher let us leave early.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'let somebody' is followed by the base form without 'to'.`,1:`'let somebody' is not followed by the -ing form.`,3:`A past form cannot follow 'let me'.`},verb:`go`,options:[`to go`,`going`,`go`,`went`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-158`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`The film was so boring that I couldn't help ___ asleep.`,explanation:`'can't help + -ing' means you cannot stop yourself: I couldn't help laughing.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'can't help' meaning 'can't stop yourself' is followed by -ing.`,1:`The base form cannot follow 'couldn't help' with this meaning.`,2:`A past form cannot follow 'help' here.`},verb:`fall`,options:[`to fall`,`fall`,`fell`,`falling`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-159`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`We're thinking of ___ to Portugal next summer.`,explanation:`Preposition + -ing: She's thinking of changing jobs.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After the preposition 'of' we use the -ing form.`,1:`A preposition cannot be followed by the base form.`,3:`A past form cannot follow a preposition.`},verb:`move`,options:[`to move`,`move`,`moving`,`moved`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-160`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`It's no use ___ to him; he never listens.`,explanation:`'It's no use + -ing' means it is pointless: It's no use crying about it.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'It's no use' is followed by the -ing form.`,1:`The base form cannot follow 'It's no use'.`,2:`A past form cannot follow 'It's no use'.`},verb:`talk`,options:[`to talk`,`talk`,`talked`,`talking`],correct:3},{id:`b2-vf-161`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`She persuaded her brother ___ the course.`,explanation:`persuade + object + to-infinitive: He persuaded me to stay.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'persuade somebody' is followed by the to-infinitive.`,2:`'persuade somebody' needs 'to' before the verb.`,3:`A past form cannot follow 'persuaded her brother'.`},verb:`join`,options:[`joining`,`to join`,`join`,`joined`],correct:1},{id:`b2-vf-162`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`I'd rather ___ at home tonight than go out.`,explanation:`'would rather + base form' shows preference: I'd rather walk than take the bus.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'would rather' is followed by the base form without 'to'.`,1:`'would rather' is not followed by -ing.`,3:`'I'd rather stayed' is wrong; the past form is only used with a different subject, e.g. 'I'd rather you stayed'.`},verb:`stay`,options:[`to stay`,`staying`,`stay`,`stayed`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-163`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`Do you fancy ___ for a walk after lunch?`,explanation:`'fancy + -ing' means to want to do something: Do you fancy watching a film?`,wrongReasons:{0:`'fancy' is followed by the -ing form, not the infinitive.`,1:`The base form cannot follow 'fancy'.`,3:`A past form cannot follow 'fancy'.`},verb:`go`,options:[`to go`,`go`,`going`,`went`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-164`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`The teacher encouraged us ___ questions.`,explanation:`encourage + object + to-infinitive: My parents encouraged me to travel.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'encourage somebody' is followed by the to-infinitive.`,1:`'encourage somebody' needs 'to' before the verb.`,3:`A past form cannot follow 'encouraged us'.`},verb:`ask`,options:[`asking`,`ask`,`to ask`,`asked`],correct:2},{id:`b2-vf-165`,type:`verb-form`,level:`B2`,topic:`verb-patterns`,prompt:`It's worth ___ the museum if you have time.`,explanation:`'be worth + -ing' means it is a good idea: The book is worth reading.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'It's worth' is followed by the -ing form.`,2:`The base form cannot follow 'worth'.`,3:`A past form cannot follow 'worth' here.`},verb:`visit`,options:[`visiting`,`to visit`,`visit`,`visited`],correct:0}]};export{e as default};